# Copy to .env, which is never committed:  cp .env.example .env
#
# There are two settings files, split by who needs the setting:
#
#   .env       what YOUR MACHINE needs to run the stack. Never deployed.
#              Read by `npm run dev` and by `docker compose`.
#
#   api/.env   what the APPLICATION needs, wherever it runs. The same file
#              exists on the servers. Read by the containers themselves.
#
# The question to ask is: does the running website need this, or only my laptop?
#
# `npm run check-env` says whether every setting is in the file that reads it,
# and `npm run dev` refuses to start when one is not. A setting in the wrong
# file is otherwise ignored in silence, and you get the default instead.
#
# If you have a .env.local from an earlier setup, move its contents here and
# delete it. `npm run dev` reads it and `docker compose` does not, so keeping
# both means the pages and your own stack can disagree about where data is.
#
# Every setting below has a working default. Change one only if you need to.

# ── Where `npm run dev` sends requests it does not answer itself ────────────
# The map tiles and the feedback API. The pages themselves always come from
# your own working folder.

# The shared development server. Nothing else needs to be running.
VITE_UPSTREAM=https://dev.ridescoredc.com

# Your own stack instead, once `npm run stack` is running:
# VITE_UPSTREAM=http://localhost:8000

# ── Your own stack, when you run `npm run stack` ────────────────────────────

# Which port on your machine reaches the database, for psql and for loading a
# published data package. Change it if 5432 is already taken -- by a Postgres
# you installed, or by another project's container. Nothing inside the stack
# uses this port, so changing it breaks nothing.
# DB_PORT=5432

# Where the database keeps its files. Point this somewhere else to keep more
# than one database side by side, or to start from an empty one without
# deleting the directory you already have. A directory named pg_data-something
# is already ignored by git.
# DB_DATA_DIR=./pg_data

# ── Where published road data comes from ────────────────────────────────────
# The defaults are in scripts/data_source.py and are right for everyone. Set
# these only to point your own machine somewhere else.
#
# To load data you built yourself, you do not need any of these -- pass the
# directories instead:
#
#   npm run data -- --package ../ridescoredc-models/out --bundle <bundle dir>
#
# DATA_RELEASES=https://github.com/civictechdc/ridescoredc-models/releases
# DATA_PACKAGE=ridescoredc-data-preview.tar.gz
# DATA_BUNDLE=ridescoredc-bundle-preview.tar.gz
# DATA_LOADER=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/.../scripts/load_package.py
